{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1590 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "705a2257e01a02bc8554bbd52159d82bde684af42403cb2bd39ac89959943fad" :edb/db-before-t 1589 :edb/db-after-t 1590 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534902 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:32:05.758Z" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068487 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534902 false] [17592186068487 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068487 1145 "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1)) &= (Ax + B)/((x^{2} + 4)) + (C)/((x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (Ax + B)(x - 1) + C(x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1$, and $C=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1))dx &= ∫(\\frac{-x + 1}{x^{2} + 4} + \\frac{1}{x - 1})dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nu = x^2+4\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= ∫(\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{du}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid x^{2} + 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx &= \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1))dx &= - ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4) + \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2}) + \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 false] [17592186068487 1145 "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+4.$ Then $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral,  we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068513 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534902 false] [17592186068513 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068513 1145 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2)) &= \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + x + 1} + \\frac{C}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - x &= (Ax + B)(x - 2) + C(x^{2} + x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2$, $B=1$, and $C=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2))dx &= ∫(\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1} + \\frac{2}{x - 2})dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx + 2∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nu = x^2+x+1\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx &= ∫(\\frac{1}{u})du \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid u \\mid \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid x^{2} + x + 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2} + x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx &= \\ln \\mid x - 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - x)/((x^{2} + x + 1)(x - 2))dx &= ∫\\frac{2x + 1}{x^{2} + x + 1}dx + 2∫\\frac{1}{x - 2}dx \\\\\n&= \\ln (x^{2} + x + 1) + 2\\ln \\mid x - 2 \\mid + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 false] [17592186068513 1145 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 1^2 - 4(1)(1) = -3 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{C}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+x+1)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get  $A=2,$ $B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-x}{(x^2+x+1)(x-2)} = \\dfrac{2x+1}{x^2+x+1} + \\dfrac{2}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration only at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}+\\frac{2}{x-2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u = x^2+x+1.$ Then $(2x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-x}{({x}^{2}+x+1)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068571 1100 "Given that\n$\\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)}\\,\\textrm d x}$." 13194139534902 false] [17592186068571 1100 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{4x^2-6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)}\\,\\textrm d x}.$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068571 1145 "First, we find the constant $A$. Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)(x^2 + 1)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x^{2} - 6 = A(x^{2} + 1) + x^{2} - 9\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(0)^{2} - 6 &= A(0^{2} + 1) + 0^{2} - 9 \\\\\n- 6 &= A - 9 \\\\\nA &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\dfrac{4x^2 - 6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}\\right) \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] & = 3\\int\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm d x + 3\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x-3} \\textrm{d}x = \\ln \\mid x-3 \\mid\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = u\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{u}\\right) \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert u\\vert \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert x^2 + 1\\vert \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\left(x^2+1\\right). \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\arctan{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(4x^{2} - 6)/((x - 3)(x^{2} + 1))dx &= 3∫\\frac{1}{x - 3}dx + ∫\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 1}dx + 3∫\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 3\\ln \\mid x - 3 \\mid + \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 1) + 3\\arctan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 false] [17592186068571 1145 "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-3)(x^2 + 1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6=A({x}^{2}+1)+{x}^{2}-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-6 & =A({0}^{2}+1)+{0}^{2}-9 \\\\ -6 & =A-9 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int \\dfrac{4x^2 - 6}{(x-3)(x^2+1)} \\, \\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{3}{x-3} + \\dfrac{x+3}{x^2 + 1}\\right) \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\n& = 3\\int\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm d x + 3\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n\t}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x-3} \\textrm{d}x = \\ln|x-3|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $x^2 + 1 = u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\textrm{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x & = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{u}\\right) \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{2} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert u\\vert \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\vert x^2 + 1\\vert \\[5pt]\n\n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\left(x^2+1\\right).\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\n}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2 + a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\arctan{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-6}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186068644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318449 13194139534902 true] [17592186068644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318477 13194139534902 true] [17592186068644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318484 13194139534902 true] [17592186068644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318498 13194139534902 true] [17592186068676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318491 13194139534902 true] [17592186068676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318505 13194139534902 true] [17592186068676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318512 13194139534902 true] [17592186068676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318526 13194139534902 true] [17592186068732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318456 13194139534902 true] [17592186068732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318463 13194139534902 true] [17592186068732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318470 13194139534902 true] [17592186068732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318519 13194139534902 true] [17592186068732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318533 13194139534902 true] [17592186069210 1091 #edb/ref 175921