)}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186318533 1113 #uuid "9eb43c75-f26e-52b2-8738-8026f1d35ba3" 13194139534902 true] [17592186318533 1145 "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x+3 & =2x(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)+3 & =2(-2)(-2+2)+D((-2)^{2}+3) \\\\ 7 & =7D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+3=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534902 true] [17592186318534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318535 13194139534902 true] [17592186318534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318536 13194139534902 true] [17592186318534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318537 13194139534902 true] [17592186318534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318538 13194139534902 true] [175